title: Drill — back to the last write
chapter: 02
type: drill
skills: write, undo, undo-branches
par: 44
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: closing without saving and reopening
open: batch.md

@brief
Eight times: append ` verified` to the line, write, append ` XXXX` on top
of it, then get the file back to the written state without retyping
anything, and quit.

The sequence to burn in is write, experiment, rewind. By rep eight,
`:earlier 1f` should feel like part of saving rather than a command you
have to remember.

@keys
| `:w` | write, creating the point you can return to |
| `:earlier 1f` | back to the state at that write |
| `:q` | quit — it will not argue, because the buffer matches the disk |

@hints
The write in the middle is what makes the rewind possible. No write, no
point to return to.
--
After `:earlier 1f` the `[+]` modified marker is gone from the status
line, so a plain `:q` is enough.

@solution
    $ a  verified <Esc> :w<Enter> $ a  XXXX <Esc> :earlier 1f<Enter> :q<Enter>

Thirty-eight keystrokes, and the twelve that matter are `:earlier 1f`.

Notice what you did not need: you did not count the changes since the
write, and you did not have to remember what the line said before. The
write recorded that for you. This is the habit the whole chapter has been
building towards — `:w` is not just insurance against a crash, it is a
marker in your undo history that you can aim at.

@start batch.md
Reprocess batch 4471

